Why: Skeid forwards nearly every client header to the node (_forward_headers only drops hop-by-hop, host, content-length, accept-encoding, expect and the correlation header). For a cloud provider that leaks client IP, cookies, trace and key ids (data minimisation). A deployment's nodes also come from a node file (ADR 0031) whose writer marks them as its own infrastructure.

Decide in an ADR (short), then build:
1. Node field trust: internal | external. Default external.
2. external: allowlist only — Content-Type, Accept, the provider protocol headers anthropic-version, anthropic-beta, OpenAI-Beta, the key Skeid itself sets for the node, and a fixed Skeid User-Agent. Everything else is dropped, explicitly X-Forwarded-*, Forwarded, X-Real-IP, Cookie, x-skeid-*, x-api-key-id, X-Request-Id, traceparent, tracestate, baggage and any deployment-prefixed headers.
3. internal: today's exclusion list stays, plus X-Forwarded-*, Forwarded, X-Real-IP and Cookie are dropped. The correlation header rule stays as it is.
4. An external node with no key source (api_key_env / api_key_ref) is a config error: Skeid refuses to start or reload (otherwise the client's credential travels to the provider). Note the incompatible change in Changes.
5. Node files: the node_files entry in the main config sets trust for its nodes (default external). A node in the file may carry trust only if it equals the entry's value; otherwise it is rejected with rule trust. trust joins the allowed fields on that condition.
6. Same rule on every route that relays to a node (chat, JSON, stream, relayed routes: audio, rerank, uploads, documents, realtime, MCP).
Test (regression): a stub provider with trust external receives none of the forbidden headers although the caller sets all of them; an internal node gets the correlation header but no IP/cookie headers; external without key fails the load; node file with trust: internal under an entry trust: internal is accepted, under default external rejected.
Done: ADR, code, tests, Changes, prove -lr t/ green.

